.TH SISTA-CURSOR 3 "2026-10-09" "Sista"
.SH NAME
sista-cursor \- clear a terminal and move its cursor
.SH LIBRARY
Sista C++ library (libSista, \fI-lSista\fP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sista/cursor.hpp>

.B "void sista::clearScreen(bool spaces = true);"
.B "sista::Cursor::Cursor();"
.B "sista::Cursor::~Cursor();"
.B "void sista::Cursor::goTo(unsigned short y, unsigned short x) const;"
.B "void sista::Cursor::goTo(Coordinates position) const;"
.B "void sista::Cursor::eraseScreen(EraseScreen mode) const;"
.B "void sista::Cursor::eraseLine(EraseLine mode,"
.B "                              bool move_cursor = true) const;"
.B "void sista::Cursor::move(MoveCursor direction,"
.B "                         unsigned short amount) const;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clearScreen()
writes terminal clearing and positioning sequences.
When
.I spaces
is false, it only returns the cursor to the top-left position.
.PP
Constructing
.B Cursor
hides the terminal cursor; destroying it shows the cursor again.
Member functions provide absolute positioning, relative movement,
erasure, and DEC or SCO save-and-restore commands.
The numeric
.B goTo(y, x)
overload uses terminal coordinates; the
.B goTo(Coordinates)
overload applies Sista's field offset.
.SH RETURN VALUE
These operations return no value.
.SH CAVEATS
Output is meaningful only on terminals that implement the emitted ANSI
sequences.
Keep cursor-owning objects scoped so their destructors restore visibility.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <sista/cursor.hpp>

int main()
{
    sista::Cursor cursor;
    cursor.goTo(4, 10);
    cursor.move(sista::MoveCursor::RIGHT, 2);
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sista-ansi (3),
.BR sista-coordinates (3),
.BR sista (7)
